Operator read arXiv:2607.02512 (Program-as-Weights), connected it to the ARC-AGI-3
Kaggle resource-restricted-hardware constraint, then asked to write it up after we
worked through which parts of the connection actually hold.

PAW compiles a fuzzy-function spec once (expensive model) into a small hybrid
artifact (discrete pseudo-program + LoRA) a tiny frozen interpreter then runs
cheaply forever after -- a 0.6B interpreter beats direct Qwen3-32B prompting at
1/50th the memory. Two distinct connections surfaced in discussion, only one
scoped here:

- Angle A (not scoped, infra-gated): GAP-4891's demo-underdetermination gap is
  exactly the failure mode PAW's own framing predicts for pure symbolic program
  induction (GAP-4's def transform(grid) approach) -- a hybrid discrete+LoRA
  artifact is a genuinely different answer, but needs a LoRA training pipeline
  Carnot doesn't have for ARC. Noted, not built.
- Angle B (this note's scope): the memory-efficiency framing doesn't transfer
  cleanly -- Carnot already solved the Kaggle 16GB constraint by picking a small
  generator up front, so PAW's memory win doesn't add new headroom. What DOES
  transfer is action-efficiency (RHAE squares the efficiency term): could a
  per-episode compile step, using only the live agent's own runtime observations
  (no source-reading), make the REST of an episode's action-selection cheaper
  than re-invoking the full generator every step.

Documented three real open questions (no-FuzzyBench-for-ARC transfer risk,
missing LoRA-training infrastructure, whether compile cost amortizes within
typical remaining-episode-length) and scoped a falsifiable FIRST pilot that
resolves only the cheapest and most decisive one -- pure log analysis + a
generic timing benchmark, zero new infrastructure, zero model calls beyond the
benchmark -- before any LoRA-training investment. Explicitly does not propose
touching the live/scored stack, does not claim to solve the separately-retired
generation-axis-exploration-signal scope, does not involve hidden-game source.

